260: \subsection{How a dose runs}
261: \label{sec:dose}
262: 
263: A dose can be requested in plain language. A person writes the request in a GitHub comment, an AI agent passes the target to the doser, and a three-phase controller on the microcontroller turns, tilts, and taps the auger until the balance reads the target (Fig.~\ref{fgr:v2dosing}). In the bulk phase the tube is tilted and the auger turns continuously; in the fine phase the tube is tilted less and the auger turns in small steps, with a settled reading after each; and in the tap phase the tube is level and solenoid taps release the last milligrams. The controller acts on the mass still to go, so the same procedure serves every target. In routine use, each powder has its own auger, filled once and stored loaded, so changing powder means swapping a tube rather than cleaning one (Fig.~\ref{fgr:v2use}). The dose lands directly in the container used for the next step; in the alloy workflow that is the cup of the atomizer, so the powder is never transferred.
