anel was measured with the tube tilted at 22.5$^\circ$. (a) Mass per auger revolution (protocol C; mean $\pm$ standard error of six revolutions; log axis). (b) Mass released by one solenoid tap (protocol E): mean $\pm$ standard deviation of eight taps, the spread to expect from a single tap, drawn only where the tap was resolved (mean more than twice its standard error, as in SI Table~\siref{tbl:v2openloop}). Each row without a bar says why. For AlSi10Mg and the coarse silicon, the balance noise with nothing moving (standard deviation 29 and 20~mg) was far larger than a tap; a tap cannot remove powder, and their negative means ($-$1.9 and $-$3.5~mg) were that noise. For CMC, the two rice flours, and the fine silicon, most single taps read 0.0--0.1~mg. Fumed silica's only run failed screening. (c) Mass against time while the auger turned continuously at 15~rpm (protocol D), for four powders from the fastest (AlSi10Mg) to a slow one (CMC). AlSi10Mg and NaCl each arrived in surges of about 200~mg every 4~s, once per auger revolution, with little in between. Gridlines every 4~s mark the auger revolutions. Blue: food-safe surrogates; orange: research-relevant powders.}
317: \label{fgr:v2openloop}
318: \end{figure*}
319: 
320: Delivery is not smooth. Balance readings streamed during continuous rotation rise in steps that recur about every 4~s at 15~rpm, once per auger revolution (Fig.~\ref{fgr:v2openloop}c). Across ten powders, the median period from autocorrelation was 3.94~s against a revolution time of 4.00~s. A single-start flight should behave this way, because each revolution carries one pocket of powder over the outlet. The pulsing limits how finely rotation alone can meter powder, and it is why the final approach to a requested mass uses taps. A single tap released between 0.2~mg (sodium alginate) and 20~mg (calcium lactate), or 0.6--10\% of one revolution's delivery (Fig.~\ref{fgr:v2openloop}b). The size of a tap does not simply follow throughput: barium chloride and calcium lactate delivered similar amounts per revolution (185 and 198~mg) but released 2.1 and 20~mg per tap. Tap increments could be resolved only on the quieter runs; on most fume-hood runs they were smaller than the bench noise.
321: 
322: Tilt is the strongest setting the controller has. For the nine powders measured at all three angles, raising the tube from horizontal to 22.5$^\circ$ increased delivery by a median factor of 4.2, and raising it further to 45$^\circ$ added a median factor of 1.2 (SI Table~\siref{tbl:v2openloop}). The cohesive powders depended most on gravity: sodium alginate delivered 15 times more per revolution at 45$^\circ$ than horizontally and white rice flour 10 times more, against about 5 times for calcium lactate and sodium sulfate. Tilt was also the only setting that moved one of the non-conveying powders at all (fine silicon, at 45$^\circ$). With the auger stopped, nothing flowed on its own: in 33 fifteen-second holds, covering all three tilts on the 11 runs recorded up to 12 August, the balance reading changed by 0.0~mg every time (protocol B). Parking the tube horizontally therefore gives a clean shut-off, at least to the resolution of the quiet-bench runs. The vibration motor could not be characterized: its driver returned an input/output error in every run that asked for protocol F. The speed sweep (protocol D) suggests that delivery rate grows more slowly than speed, with a median exponent of 0.78 across ten powders for rate $\propto$ speed$^{\alpha}$ over 15--90~rpm, but each speed was measured once, in a fixed order, and a firmware timing error let the auger turn 15--54\% further than commanded, so we treat these exponents as an indication only.
323: 
324: The two ends of the range are consistent with particle shape. Gas-atomized AlSi10Mg consists of near-spherical particles in a narrow size range, the properties L-PBF requires, and it was the fastest powder tested. The fine silicon is milled, angular, and smaller than 44~$\mu$m (it passes a 325-mesh sieve). It did not move at all at 0$^\circ$ or 22.5$^\circ$, and at 45$^\circ$ it produced a single 7~mg pulse in six revolutions. The coarse silicon, the same material at roughly 75--150~$\mu$m, delivered 211~mg per revolution. Forces that make particles stick together matter more relative to their weight as particles get smaller, which fits this sharp difference between the two silicon grades. We read the three powders that conveyed no measurable amount as a limit of this auger geometry rather than a fixed property of the powders; a wider flight, a larger pitch, or a taller fin would move that limit. The failures also differ in kind. Fine silicon moved slightly at the steepest tilt, so for it the driving force was too weak to overcome cohesion. Fumed silica, which is extremely light and fluffy, never delivered more than a few milligrams per revolution at any tilt and varied wildly from one revolution to the next, which points instead to trapped air.
